Before you come in
You’re ready for the session if you can:

store a value in an object with &lt;- and check its type with class();
create a vector with c(), and use na.rm = TRUE when it contains missing values;
pick values out of a vector by position, or by a condition, using square brackets.

In the session you will do all of this in RStudio, and learn to use R’s own help pages. Go to the week 1 page to download the student pack before you arrive.

Categorical variables: counts and percentages
For a categorical variable, count() gives the number in each category, and mutate() can turn the counts into percentages:

dia |&gt;
  count(smoker) |&gt;
  mutate(percent = 100 * n / sum(n))

# A tibble: 2 × 3
  smoker     n percent
  &lt;fct&gt;  &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;
1 No       158    65.8
2 Yes       82    34.2


sum(n) is the total of the n column (240), so each count is divided by the total and multiplied by 100.
With two variables, you usually want percentages within each group: “what percentage of each group smokes?”, rather than “what percentage of all patients are smokers in usual care?”. Group before calculating the percentage:

dia |&gt;
  count(group, smoker) |&gt;
  group_by(group) |&gt;
  mutate(percent = 100 * n / sum(n)) |&gt;
  ungroup()

# A tibble: 4 × 4
  group               smoker     n percent
  &lt;fct&gt;               &lt;fct&gt;  &lt;int&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Usual care          No        83    66.9
2 Usual care          Yes       41    33.1
3 Intensive lifestyle No        75    64.7
4 Intensive lifestyle Yes       41    35.3


Within each group, sum(n) is now that group’s total, so the two percentages for each group add up to 100.

Activity 10 (optional): On your own
If you finish early, or want more practice after the session, here is a second dataset with the same kinds of problem. bootcamp_messy_clinic.csv comes from a (simulated) six-month trial of a lifestyle programme in 260 adults with raised blood pressure, compared with a control group. It records systolic blood pressure (SBP, mmHg) and total cholesterol (mmol/L) at baseline, 3 months and 6 months. This time no code is provided.
Write code, in as many chunks as you like, that:

imports the data and identifies its problems;
tidies the column names, and renames the measurement columns to a consistent pattern;
recodes sex, smoking status and group (as factors, with readable labels), and converts age to a number;
finds any missing-value codes and replaces them with NA (check every measurement column, not just one);
reshapes the SBP data to long format, then calculates and plots mean SBP over time in each group.

Finish by writing two or three sentences describing what you found.

